NTPCTL(8)                 BSD System Manager's Manual                NTPCTL(8)

NNAAMMEE
     nnttppccttll — control the NTP daemon

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     nnttppccttll --ss aallll | ppeeeerrss | SSeennssoorrss | ssttaattuuss

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The nnttppccttll program displays information about the running ntpd(8) daemon.

     The options are as follows:

     --ss aallll | ppeeeerrss | SSeennssoorrss | ssttaattuuss
                   Used to display information about the running daemon.  Key‐
                   words may be abbreviated.

                   aallll shows all data available.

                   ppeeeerrss shows the following information about each peer:
                   weight, trustlevel, stratum, number of seconds until the
                   next poll, polling interval in seconds, and offset, network
                   delay and network jitter in milliseconds.  When the system
                   clock is synced to a peer, an asterisk is displayed to the
                   left of the weight column for that peer.

                   SSeennssoorrss shows the following information about each sensor:
                   weight, sensor "good" status, stratum, and offset and the
                   configured correction in milliseconds.  When the system
                   clock is synced to a sensor, an asterisk is displayed to
                   the left of the weight column for that sensor.

                   ssttaattuuss shows the status of peers and sensors, and whether
                   the system clock is synced.  When the system clock is
                   synced, the stratum is displayed.  When the system clock is
                   not synced, the offset of the system clock, as reported by
                   the adjtime(2) system call, is displayed.  When the median
                   constraint is set, the offset to the local time is dis‐
                   played.

FFIILLEESS
     /var/run/ntpd.sock     Socket file for communication with ntpd(8).

SSEEEE AALLSSOO
     adjtime(2), ntpd.conf(5), ntpd(8)

HHIISSTTOORRYY
     The nnttppccttll program first appeared in OpenBSD 5.5.

